SM(525,{"n":"Romans 15","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 15.mp3","d":2266.3,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"And uh when we get started, in just a moment we'll be picking up in St."],[25.6,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, uh, chapter 15, and we will most likely be finishing up."],[32.72,"Uh Romans tonight."],[34.24,"A lot of chapter 16 is St."],[37.76,"Paul sending his various greetings that we'll discuss briefly, but we'll not be subject to subject to the same level of uh inquiry as as uh some of the other passages we've covered."],[51.44,"So just as a as a catch up, back at the beginning of chapter twelve, we saw that there was sort of this transition."],[58.0,"We talked about how there's that transition in all of St."],[60.28,"Paul's epistles, where there's usually marked by the word therefore, where Saint Paul finishes having sort of laid out the theological points that he wants to make, and then he gets to directly applying them to the people to whom he's writing."],[76.78,"Here's why I've been telling you all this."],[79.82,"Because as we said way back in the long ago time on the first Bible study on the St."],[85.18,"Paul's epistle to the Romans, contrary to what some of particularly our Protestant friends would say, uh St."],[92.94,"Paul's letters are letters, they're occasional documents."],[96.22,"They're not just him sitting down to write a treatise on theology."],[101.1,"Treatises existed in the ancient world."],[104.14,"We've got lots of them."],[105.46,"Oh, yeah. By pagan philosophers, by Christians, by all kinds of people, you know, on fill in the topic."],[114.92,"On the soul, on the resurrection, on whatever."],[118.76,"Philo of Alexandria, who lived a little bit before Saint Paul, was a Jewish author who wrote a number of treatises on topics like that."],[130.04,"He would write a treatise, you know, on the Decalogue, on the Ten Commandments, on the sacrifices of the temple, on so Saint Paul, if he had wanted to write such things, could have written such things."],[142.84,"If he did, we don't have any of them."],[146.52,"And they aren't in the scriptures."],[148.04,"So what's in the scriptures are the letters that he wrote to churches that are dealing with specific problems and specific situations at a specific time and place."],[157.36,"That's one of the reasons why all those greetings at the end are important, is that they remind us of that."],[162.49,"That these are written to actual humans who are living their actual human life."],[166.73,"And everything St."],[167.85,"Paul says in this letter is aimed at that."],[170.81,"So the reason he made the specific theological points he made in his epistle to the Romans, and he's going to make different points in other letters, is that they were specifically relevant to what was going on in Rome."],[184.89,"And we've talked several times about what that was, about the expulsion of of the Jewish people and then their return to Rome, and then St."],[193.89,"Paul's concern that because of their time away there might be there might be a split into two different churches one church of Jewish Christian faithful and one church of Gentile non-Jewish Christian faithful."],[209.01,"And he wanted to make sure that didn't happen."],[211.57,"And we saw as we went through those theological sections how he talked about Israel, what it means to be part of Israel, that that's not the same thing as being Jewish and how that all works."],[223.73,"And now, when we've gotten into this application section, as we've seen, he's been focusing very heavily on the unity of the community, the Christian community in Rome, and how that unity is manifest in a very practical way."],[239.21,"Through showing love for one another, helping one another, bearing with one another's weaknesses, being willing to give up our own quote-unquote rights in order to help someone else or in favor of someone else, not uh putting stumbling blocks in other Christians' paths, loving our enemies."],[259.55,"All these things have all been focused at that theme of unity."],[263.79,"He's argued theologically, you're all one people in Christ, and now he's saying, now this is what that looks like."],[270.91,"And this is what this means you need to do every day."],[274.93,"And every week as you gather for worship."],[277.17,"This is what you need to do."],[279.01,"So that's where we're picking up here in chapter 15."],[283.45,"So, unless there are any other questions or comments or recriminations or anything else, we'll go ahead and get started in chapter 15, verse 1."],[296.49,"Okay. We then who are strong ought to bear with the scruples of the weak and not to please ourselves."],[306.01,"So this is referring back to what we just had read last time, where St."],[312.17,"Paul was talking about the fact that yes, we have rights, yes, we're free as Christians, but we should never use our freedom or our rights in a way that might spiritually or in any other way harm our fellow Christians."],[327.85,"So he gave specifically about food"],[334.85,"Food. He said some of you still do that, some of you don't."],[347.54,"Um, whichever side you fall on, you shouldn't exercise that."],[352.26,"If you're someone who does keep the Jewish Sabbath, so you're going to the synagogue on the Sabbath, and you're going to uh the Christian community's gathering for the Eucharist on Sunday, great."],[364.66,"But if there's, say, a Gentile Christian who doesn't go to the synagogue, the Jewish Sabbath, you shouldn't judge him. for not doing that"],[374.78,"Right? And likewise the Gentile Christian who doesn't do that shouldn't criticize the one who does, or try and talk him out of it."],[384.0,"Or get him to change, get him to offend against his own conscience."],[388.48,"So that was one example."],[390.08,"The eating of different foods, obviously, there were there were Christians who still kept kosher, there were Christians who didn't."],[396.88,"St. Paul said, Hey, as far as God is concerned, right now, everything has been made clean."],[406.25,"It's not a sin to eat whatever."],[408.49,"But if someone's way of being faithful to God is expressed in their diet, and so you know, they're vegetarians. because they don't want to eat meat offered to idols, potentially meat offered to idols in the marketplace"],[421.07,"They don't want to uh eat anything unclean, let them be, you know."],[428.11,"Um, but that person should not judge their brothers and sisters in Christ who don't who choose not to follow that way."],[439.07,"So uh this is what he's referring back to uh when he says here that we ought to bear with it translated scruples, which isn't the the uh I'm almost sure that it doesn't capture the original meaning."],[454.03,"Well, yeah. Um scruple, it's really it's really dealing with the the the um opinion isn't really right word either."],[460.51,"uh I know hang up with me."],[468.13,"The the it's having to do with um the place where the original word would have been used would have been like, for example, the Stoics practice sir a certain way of life."],[484.67,"So this would be referring to principles of a certain way of life."],[488.35,"Okay. Things that you've chosen to do and not do."],[491.17,"Now, obviously, St."],[492.37,"Paul has said there's a Christian way of life already, but within that, there's variations for individual people."],[501.78,"Um, especially when you're dealing with people. from who have come out of two different cultures"],[509.5,"Um, so Saint Paul never says, never says, and remember in the book of Acts, he was he very passionately defended himself against this actual accusation."],[519.66,"He never says that Jewish people should should stop following the things in the Torah."],[524.54,"He never says, Oh, you need to trim the edge of your beard now."],[527.36,"He never says, Oh, you need to go and eat shrimp and pork."],[532.3,"To prove that you're, you know, a Christian now or something."],[536.08,"He never does that."],[537.04,"But at the same time, he also violently opposes anyone trying to force the Gentiles to do all those things as a condition of being part of the of the Christian community."],[549.72,"So he says in verse 2, let each of us please his neighbor for his good, leading to edification."],[555.74,"So this is he's getting down to the underlying principle."],[559.59,"That we as Christians don't live to please ourselves."],[566.69,"Or what I think is gonna be beneficial to me."],[569.59,"I may love a good pork sandwich."],[573.24,"But if if my neighbor doesn't eat pork, I don't have to eat it in front of him."],[579.74,"I can go to my home and eat whatever I want."],[582.46,"When I'm with him, I can share fellowship with him and not do anything to offend him purposefully."],[590.9,"And so then the flip side of that is that each of us seeks to please our neighbor."],[597.11,"And then notice he further defines that."],[599.43,"Please does not just mean make happy."],[601.51,"Uh-huh. Because there are lots of sinful things that we can do that might that might please someone."],[606.83,"But please them for their good."],[610.83,"Leading to edification, leading to them being built up, leading to the helping them come to maturity."],[616.13,"So this that's an objective thing."],[618.59,"It's not just what will make them happy in the short term."],[621.47,"If someone's an addict, giving them drugs would please them."],[625.16,"But we don't do that because that's not for their good."],[630.81,"For even Christ did not please himself, but as it is written, the reproaches of those who approached you fell on me."],[640.65,"So he says, look, Christ, when he came."],[643.15,"He came not to be served, but to serve and to give his life as a ransom for many."],[653.55,"But he came for our sakes."],[656.03,"Not again to please us and make us happy."],[658.67,"Not so we'd be wealthy and healthy and wise, contra some television preachers."],[665.87,"But he came, he came uh for our good, uh, to save us."],[673.07,"Okay. And uh St."],[679.99,"Paul cites there um Psalm 69 verse 9, and we won't go back to the whole of Psalm 69, um, but Psalm 69 uh is a psalm that all the way through is talking about the Messiah, it's talking about Christ uh as the one who suffers for the sake of his people."],[702.87,"That's what St."],[703.51,"Paul is alluding to here."],[704.71,"Because as we've commented several times."],[707.17,"There are no chapters and verses at this time in history."],[712.33,"So you refer to something by quoting a line of it, and quoting that first line then is supposed to direct. you to the rest"],[731.49,"Meant that people had to know the scriptures much better than we do now."],[735.73,"In the same way that when I was a kid, I knew everybody's phone number."],[740.29,"You know, I had memorized it."],[741.97,"Now I don't know hardly anybody's phone number because they're all programmed in."],[750.17,"Okay, but why is it important that he quotes uh Psalm 69?"],[753.37,"Because he says, For whatever things were written before were written for our learning, that we, through the patience and comfort of the scriptures, might have hope."],[764.01,"Now, um a note here, the word that's translated scriptures and the word that's translated uh whatever things were written, the same word."],[774.49,"That's what scriptures means."],[780.02,"And uh I'm sure I've talked about in the past, but I think it's been a while, that the phrase it is written, is uh doesn't sort of resonate with us today the way it resonated in the first century, because of course nowadays lots of things are written."],[797.87,"Even without taking the internet into account where all kinds of things are are written."],[802.83,"You know, there are libraries full of books that were written on all kinds of things, and a lot of them are utter nonsense."],[807.79,"utter nonsense. Pretty much the whole internet is, uh, except for ancient faith ministries"],[813.81,"Yes, yes, yes."],[816.21,"Uh but uh so uh for us something being written down."],[825.17,"Well, okay, you know, I wrote something on a napkin last night, who cares?"],[829.38,"But at this time in history, remember that this was great to to write something was of great expense."],[836.9,"So uh a scroll a copy of uh of the of St."],[841.14,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans that we've been reading, which isn't that long of a book."],[844.98,"I know we've been taking a long time, but is it isn't that long of a book, even for biblical books."],[850.02,"But a copy of it would have cost in the first century the equivalent of about $5,000 a day."],[857.1,"So if a relatively short text cost $5,000, you can imagine what it is written meant."],[867.64,"It meant that not only had this been written down at great expense, but it had been copied and recopied and recopied at great expense through centuries, through history to come to us now."],[878.68,"So something being written down at this time in history is sort of it's sort of like a monument."],[884.68,"Uh-huh It means that it is something of great significance."],[889.06,"Okay Um the other thing to note here is that uh Saint Paul here does not make any distinction about canon."],[896.34,"There isn't one"],[900.19,"He says whatever was written."],[904.09,"He's not naming specific books."],[906.25,"He doesn't say the Torah."],[908.17,"He doesn't say the law and the prophets, even."],[910.41,"The Torah and the Prophets, which he could have said."],[912.73,"He says whatever was written."],[915.13,"We've seen a couple times in Romans, and we'll be seeing a lot more as we go through 1 and 2 Corinthians next, some of the other books that St."],[922.57,"Paul is constantly quoting and referencing literature that didn't end up being part of most people's Old Testament."],[931.69,"Does he sometimes quote things that they don't have at all anymore?"],[935.37,"Well, potentially, it's hard to tell."],[937.37,"Yeah. So but there have been things that we used to think that we've discovered are quotes, I should say."],[947.01,"Yeah. Because we've recovered some text or other."],[949.73,"Uh-huh. And now we know, oh, that's what that is."],[953.75,"He's referencing this or that."],[958.23,"So there is not, when when St."],[961.11,"Paul here talking when they translate it the scriptures as they have in the Orthodox Study Bible, and put a capital S on it, that makes us think of the Bible."],[968.55,"Or it makes us think of the Old Testament."],[971.41,"But uh St."],[973.17,"Paul isn't directly referencing."],[975.41,"Like I said, he doesn't say the law and the prophets."],[978.63,"So he's when he speaks here of the things that were written, he's talking about a wide range of texts and literature and traditions that he's he and they have received."],[996.19,"And notice that he says, We're written for our learning and we."],[1004.91,"Our and we includes both the members of the community from a Jewish background and the members of the community from a Gentile background."],[1016.27,"But he's usually quoting Jewish sources."],[1020.24,"But he's saying those belong to the Gentiles just as much as they belong."],[1026.0,"Okay. Just as much as they belong to the Jewish members."],[1030.22,"This is part of their heritage because they've been grafted in to Israel."],[1035.66,"I assume that he never quotes a Greek or Roman."],[1041.98,"Actually, he does it a couple places."],[1044.86,"We've already seen one."],[1045.82,"Acts 17 is the most famous one."],[1047.74,"Uh huh. In him we live and move and have our being."],[1050.54,"That's from a hymn to Zeus."],[1052.62,"No joke. Wow."],[1054.7,"He probably told us that."],[1056.14,"Yeah, he he quotes, and there are a couple places where he quotes Stoic philosophers."],[1061.98,"So, yeah, he does."],[1063.26,"Okay. But he never he never uses this it is written phrase with that stuff."],[1067.98,"Uh-huh. Only with what he's received through the traditions of Israel."],[1072.22,"the And he considers Gentiles part of Israel, so this is their heritage too."],[1074.5,"Uh-huh. And it's our heritage too."],[1081.3,"Doesn't matter what ethnicity we are we are."],[1084.02,"When we say, Blessed art thou, God of our fathers, that means Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, but they're our fathers too."],[1091.01,"They're our fathers because we're members of the church."],[1095.51,"Which is Israel."],[1097.27,"Not a new Israel."],[1099.83,"It's Israel. We've been grafted in."],[1102.95,"So he's St."],[1104.31,"Paul is arguing here that all of that heritage, all of that literary heritage of Israel, coming right up to the present day, including this epistle that he's writing."],[1115.64,"Is all part of the heritage of the entire community there."],[1121.64,"And he's exemplified that."],[1123.32,"Notice it starts with four, because."],[1126.28,"Because what's he just done?"],[1129.0,"He's quoted Isaiah."],[1130.44,"Yeah. Or not Isaiah, sorry, Psalm 69."],[1132.92,"He's quoted Psalm 69."],[1135.45,"To a group of Jews and Gentiles, saying this was written to you about Christ and about how you should live in Christ."],[1142.89,"This wasn't written to a bunch of people a long time ago who you may or may not be descended from."],[1149.61,"But this was written for you."],[1151.45,"This is your heritage."],[1156.17,"Now may the God of patience and comfort grant you to be like-minded toward one another, according to Christ Jesus, that you may with one mind and one mouth glorify the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1169.17,"Now you may recognize that what's translated here, one mind and one mouth, because a version of this is used in the liturgy."],[1177.89,"I can't remember where that."],[1187.49,"Before the hymn be the. to sing during Kiss of Peace"],[1195.13,"The Holy Spirit is added there too."],[1198.89,"But just looking at the language that St."],[1200.97,"Paul uses here, God is the God of patience and comfort."],[1207.31,"Because again, it's reminding them."],[1209.71,"He's just said you need to be like Christ."],[1212.27,"He's saying you need to be like God."],[1214.19,"And God is a God of patience and comfort."],[1216.91,"And so, how should you behave towards one another?"],[1219.63,"Patience and comfort."],[1220.59,"You should be patient with one another."],[1222.19,"You should be you should seek to comfort one another."],[1226.11,"And the word translated there, comfort, is the same word that's used for the Holy Spirit as the comforter."],[1232.51,"And so comfort is not the best translation."],[1235.51,"Uh, exhort is a good translation."],[1239.79,"It's tying it back to the idea of building each other up and doing good for each other, advocating for each other."],[1246.63,"Be like-minded, according to Christ Jesus, that you may with one mind and one mouth glorify the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1255.83,"Now, there's a lot of weird, weird textual variance in that last verse in verse 6."],[1263.19,"So there's a question as to whether it says the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ in the Greek, or whether it says God the Father and our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1274.15,"Uh-huh. So either is possible."],[1285.03,"Neither of those would be heretical."],[1288.63,"It's very common for St."],[1290.47,"Paul to use the phrase God the Father and our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1293.27,"the He uses that phrase all time."],[1300.75,"But that could work either way."],[1302.64,"You could say, well, he uses that all the time, so that's probably what he's saying now."],[1306.48,"Or you could say, well, he uses that all the time, so whoever was making the copy at some point thought that's what wasn't reading closely and thought that's what it said."],[1316.48,"Uh-huh. But uh this also, and there are people who will say, well, saying that he's the God of our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1328.44,"That's implying that Jesus isn't God."],[1330.66,"Now we've already seen throughout Romans that St."],[1333.38,"Paul has equated Jesus as God."],[1336.34,"But more to the point, Christ himself remember said, I am ascending to."],[1341.34,"And this is in St."],[1342.62,"John's Gospel, where St."],[1344.46,"John unambiguously presents Christ as God."],[1347.12,"Everyone agrees on that."],[1348.49,"And he says to uh Saint Mary Magdalene, remember, he says, Don't hold on to me, for I'm ascending to my Father, your Father, my God, your God."],[1361.53,"Jesus Christ as the incarnate second person of the Trinity."],[1366.73,"It's not an atheist."],[1369.37,"No. And in the Gospels, he doesn't just talk to himself when he prays."],[1375.98,"Uh-huh. He spe speaksaks to to he God the Father."],[1382.45,"And so it is not inappropriate for Saint Paul, nor is it a denial that Christ is God to say that the Father is the God of our of our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1395.57,"And this is this is, by the way, one of the things that points to that understanding is the fact that this is a very rare place where Saint Paul uses the name Jesus."],[1405.41,"I don't know if you've noticed, but he he talks about Christ all the time."],[1409.73,"He refers to him as just Christ."],[1411.89,"It's very rare that he inserts the name Jesus."],[1416.13,"And when he so when he does, it's important."],[1419.01,"And so one of the obvious reasons to insert it here in this phrase is that it's pointing to Jesus Christ, the incarnate Lord."],[1423.33,"Uh-huh And to that, to that understanding."],[1433.61,"Verse 7, therefore receive one another just as Christ also received us to the glory of God."],[1439.29,"He's keeping that theme."],[1441.75,"That we need to be like Christ in our behavior toward one another."],[1445.43,"He has received us."],[1447.68,"Whether you're a murderer like St."],[1450.16,"Paul. Whether you were a Jewish person who fell short of keeping the Torah, whether you were a Gentile who was far off and who was a pagan."],[1462.12,"Christ has received all of us."],[1464.76,"And so we need to receive one another. regardless"],[1468.88,"Now I say that Jesus Christ has become a servant to the circumcision for the truth of God to confirm the promise made to the fathers, and that the Gentiles might glorify God for his mercy."],[1480.8,"Okay, that doesn't make any sense."],[1482.56,"Yeah. So the circumcision, is a way that not often, we'll see this again in Galatians, is a way that Saint Paul refers to Christians of Jewish descent and tradition."],[1499.82,"Uh-huh. Because they're Christians who are circumcised."],[1503.9,"Uh-huh. He can't."],[1507.12,"What why would he use that term?"],[1508.8,"Well, what if he said Jews?"],[1511.0,"Christ is a servant to the Jews."],[1513.48,"Well, a lot of them have rejected him."],[1515.32,"Uh-huh. And so there isn't a term like Jewish Christians."],[1521.64,"So this is, he's saying, among you, this is his way of within the Roman Christian community, there are the group who are circumcised, who came out of this Jewish tradition and background."],[1535.16,"And there are those of you who are Gentiles."],[1537.72,"And so those are the two groups he cites here."],[1540.44,"Jesus Christ has become a servant to the circumcision for the truth of God to confirm the promises made to the fathers."],[1547.4,"So that all requires."],[1548.69,"For the truth of God, meaning what?"],[1551.13,"God made promises."],[1552.65,"Uh-huh. In the Old Testament, in the Hebrew scriptures."],[1554.51,"God made promises to Abraham and his descendants"],[1560.29,"He made promises in the Torah."],[1561.89,"He made all these promises."],[1563.25,"And so St."],[1564.05,"Paul is here saying, Jesus Christ is on one hand, the fulfillment of all those promises that were made to them."],[1574.07,"On one hand."],[1575.27,"And then on the other hand, that the Gentiles might glorify God for his mercy."],[1580.87,"So for those of you who are from a Jewish background, he's the fulfillment of all those promises made to your fathers."],[1588.55,"To the Gentiles, he's the mercy of God in receiving you back."],[1593.59,"Bringing you, bringing you to the covenant, so that you can also worship and glorify the true God."],[1598.31,"So Christ is both of those to the two groups"],[1604.59,"As it is written, there's the phrase again."],[1608.11,"For this reason I will confess to you among the Gentiles and sing to your name."],[1613.79,"And that is from 2 Samuel 22, verse 50, or 2nd Kingdoms, if you're using the Orthodox Study Bible."],[1624.03,"And Psalm 18, verse 49."],[1626.59,"Now remember, Gentiles, really means nations."],[1632.05,"Most English translations are not really good at being consistent about this."],[1636.13,"When it's referring to people, when it's referring to a person who's not a Jew, you should translate it Gentile."],[1642.3,"Because that makes sense."],[1643.54,"That's the idea."],[1645.14,"When it's talking like this, I'll confess your name among the Gentiles."],[1648.84,"It would really be better to translate it nations."],[1651.86,"I'll confess your name among the nations."],[1654.66,"Lots of psalms and other things refer to the nations."],[1658.42,"Is that the same word?"],[1659.6,"Uh huh. Okay."],[1660.54,"Why do the nations rage on the heathen plot in vain?"],[1663.59,"You wouldn't translate that."],[1664.47,"Why do the Gentiles in our ears that would change the meaning dramatically?"],[1671.25,"So this is for this reason I will confess to you among the Gentiles and sing to your name."],[1676.37,"And again, I'm going to retranslate."],[1679.33,"Rejoice, O nations with his people."],[1682.79,"Which is Deuteronomy 32, verse 43."],[1685.07,"And again, this is from Psalm 117"],[1690.23,"Praise the Lord all you nations, laud him all you peoples."],[1695.25,"Which is one of the Psalms, that verse is quoted from the Psalms in Vespers."],[1701.73,"And this again, praise, praise him, all ye nations."],[1705.01,"It's nations. Yeah, they translate it nations."],[1707.81,"Yeah. And again, Isaiah says, There shall be a root of Jesse, and he who shall rise to reign over the nations, in him the nations shall hope."],[1718.53,"And that's uh Isaiah 11, verse 10."],[1722.69,"So all of these passages, remember, he's not just quoting proof texting a verse, he's referring to a passage."],[1728.93,"All of these passages are talking about the fact that God's plan, the earliest of these in terms of time, would be Deuteronomy 32, uh verse 43."],[1740.25,"And I mean earliest in terms of time in all senses, because Deuteronomy 32 is one of the oldest pieces of text in in the Bible."],[1748.81,"Really? Uh so much so that Deuteronomy 32 is really hard to translate."],[1754.33,"Uh-huh. Uh, because a lot of the words in it we just don't have anywhere else in the scriptures, and they're archaic Hebrew words uh that don't exist."],[1765.61,"It's so ancient that the people who translated, well, in this case it actually was the 70, uh the Septuagint, who translated Deuteronomy into Greek. didn't know how to translate chunks of Deuteronomy 32"],[1778.29,"The Greek is weird because there were the words were so old they didn't even know."],[1783.79,"And we're talking about the third century BC."],[1786.43,"It was already lost, sort of to time."],[1789.07,"So this is one of the earliest texts there is."],[1792.19,"And this is talking about God's purposes in redeeming Israel."],[1798.51,"But Deuteronomy 32 begins with by describing what happened at the Tower of Babel when God rejected the nations."],[1806.27,"He scattered them."],[1807.48,"You get the Table of nations."],[1808.48,"There's seventy nations, and he assigned them to other spiritual powers who they ended up worshiping."],[1815.64,"And so that's where it starts."],[1817.8,"Then it talks about him creating. Israel"],[1820.3,"God doesn't choose one of those seventy nations to be his, he creates a new one by grabbing Abraham out of one of them and making a nation out of them and then bringing them together as a nation as he brought them out of Egypt."],[1833.52,"But Deuteronomy 32, so from the very beginning, this is Moses' final sermon."],[1839.76,"From the very beginning, his purpose in doing that was why?"],[1844.48,"Ultimately, to bring those nations back to worship him."],[1848.72,"Which one of these is the one from Deuteronomy?"],[1851.04,"That's the uh in verse 11."],[1853.2,"Okay. Praise the Lord all you nations, law in him all you peoples."],[1856.72,"So the purpose for creating Israel was to bring to bring uh all of the nations back."],[1863.28,"Let me ask you about Deuteronomy 32"],[1866.76,"This must have been, since you specifically say in that chapter, this must have been an old document that was put in to by the writer of Deuteronomy who said, Well, this says what I mean, so I'm just gonna quote"],[1883.06,"The the Torah came together over time."],[1888.34,"Okay, we know that for sure."],[1890.1,"Yeah. I was raised on the JDP."],[1893.22,"Yeah, well that yeah, that's going a little far."],[1896.34,"But but there's sort of the the very rigid view that, well, Moses wrote it."],[1902.74,"The problem with, well, Moses wrote it, there's a few of 'em."],[1906.1,"One of them is the language it's written in did.n't exist when Moses lived"],[1909.9,"It's not to say that Moses didn't write something."],[1912.68,"But what we have now is not word for word something that Moses wrote, because the language didn't exist."],[1918.28,"There are a whole bunch of places in the Torah where place names have been updated."],[1926.84,"And I don't mean we think place names have been updated."],[1929.96,"I mean it says, you know, this place, which used to be called this other name."],[1935.08,"Yeah, uh-huh. I remember those."],[1936.89,"And the older names are contemporaneous with what's being described."],[1942.49,"And then there's a newer name."],[1944.41,"So there was some."],[1946.13,"All this adds up to whatever Moses wrote."],[1949.69,"There were people in later centuries who editeded it, translat it into a more modern form of Hebrew."],[1957.91,"Updated those plates' names and made those notes."],[1960.79,"Obviously, someone other than Moses wrote about the death of Moses and what happened immediately afterwards at the end of Deuteronomy."],[1968.47,"So when I talk about Deuteronomy 32 being one of the oldest things in the scriptures and being one of the oldest parts of the Torah, that means that it's a piece of text that goes back to the period when Moses would have lived."],[1984.63,"And that's there relatively intact, uh, as evidenced by the fact that it's so hard to translate and it's so archaic that it hasn't been updated by anybody."],[1996.55,"As near as we can tell"],[2000.19,"Now, when I say updated, I mean updated around a thousand BC, between a thousand and nine hundred BC, which is when most of those place names and that kind of thing have been updated."],[2010.91,"So, yeah, the the Torah as we have it now is the result, it is a tradition."],[2017.73,"It's a tradition."],[2018.93,"Most conservative uh biblical scholars now talk about the mosaic origin of the Pentateuch, that the traditions in the Torah, in the Pentateuch, ultimately have their origin with Moses and in his time period."],[2034.19,"But as we have it now, there's been this long process of revision and addition and incorporation. of other traditions and that kind of thing to produce what we have now"],[2044.71,"And this isn't really a problem for us as Orthodox Christians, because there are a whole bunch of Orthodox."],[2049.67,"Now, this is problematic, and I don't want to go down this rabble hole, but there's a whole bunch of Orthodox Christians who will tell you that the Greek version is the one that's canonical."],[2057.67,"Well, the Greek version was translated in 300 BC."],[2061.03,"Uh-huh. So if the canonical version didn't exist before 300 BC, you kind of have to argue for a tradition anyway."],[2067.75,"Yeah. Uh-huh. So this isn't a problem for us."],[2070.8,"It's, in fact, it it very much fits with the Orthodox understanding of tradition."],[2076.8,"That the scriptures themselves are a tradition."],[2079.61,"The old the Old Testament in particular developed over time."],[2083.53,"The New Testament is a little different, uh, in that you know, Saint Paul wrote his letters, and we've got pretty good evidence that what we have now is pretty much exactly what he wrote."],[2095.41,"So that's sort of a different case."],[2097.49,"But we're talking about the Old Testament, especially like the Torah, the oldest parts of the Old Testament."],[2103.57,"These have gone through this process."],[2107.73,"And that's fine because the Holy Spirit can guide a process, just like he guides the original author or authors."],[2117.73,"Um, and we believe that in terms of the New Testament, anyway, because the New Testament had to get from St."],[2123.41,"Paul 2,000 years ago to us today."],[2128.39,"And so the Holy Spirit had to take care of that process too to make sure that it was handed down and copied and that we received what God wanted us to have."],[2138.03,"But so yeah, this is very, but so from the beginning, St."],[2142.67,"Paul is saying, Israel was not an end in itself contra some of his opponents."],[2149.39,"It was never God's intent that he would only be the God of Israel, as opposed to the nations."],[2158.35,"It was always his intent that through Israel."],[2161.73,"Israel who produces."],[2163.43,"Jesus Christ is born as a Galilean, technically not a Jew, but Galilean."],[2170.61,"Is born from this nation."],[2172.21,"this And so through nation, he is calling all of the nations back."],[2179.45,"Okay. And to make sure you get the point about Christ, that last quote from Isaiah, there shall be a root of Jesse."],[2187.38,"A descendant of David."],[2188.74,"This is talking about the Messiah."],[2190.34,"This is talking about a king who will be born from his."],[2192.9,"And it's through him, he shall rise, which St."],[2198.18,"Paul is maybe using a little less metaphorically than it may have been read before."],[2203.38,"It probably would have been read before his rise as in rise to be king."],[2207.0,"And Christ very literally rose to be king, to be a third as king, to reign over the nations, and in him the nations shall hope."],[2219.36,"So he's drawn on all these different parts of the Hebrew scriptures from the very oldest, the prophets, the Psalms, to uh to show that this was the plan all along."],[2231.6,"This was God's plan all along."],[2232.96,"This is what he was doing throughout the Hebrew scriptures, and now it's come to this culmination in Christ and in the community of Christians in Rome."],[2243.92,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2251.28,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2256.64,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15: finishing the epistle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tonight: <b>Romans 15</b>, probably <b>finishing Romans</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Much of <b>ch. 16</b> = Paul's <b>greetings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">discussed briefly - not the same level of inquiry as other passages</li><li class=\"d0\">Catch-up: <b>ch. 12</b> = the transition</li><li class=\"d1\">every Pauline epistle: marked by the word <b>\"therefore\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul done laying out theology → <b>applies it directly</b> to the people he writes to</li></ul>"},{"t":76.78,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's letters are letters, not treatises","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Way back in first Romans study: Paul's letters are <b>occasional documents</b></li><li class=\"d1\">contrary to some <b>Protestant friends</b> - not him sitting down to write a <b>treatise on theology</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Treatises existed in the ancient world - lots of them</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan philosophers, Christians: \"on the soul, on the resurrection, on whatever\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Philo of Alexandria</b> - a bit before Paul, Jewish - treatises on the <b>Decalogue</b>, <b>temple sacrifices</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If Paul wanted to write such things he could have</li><li class=\"d1\">if he did, <b>we don't have any</b> - not in the scriptures</li><li class=\"d0\">What we have = <b>letters to churches</b> facing <b>specific problems, specific time and place</b></li></ul>"},{"t":157.36,"lv":3,"h":"Why the greetings matter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All those greetings at the end remind us: written to <b>actual humans living actual lives</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Everything Paul says is aimed at that</li><li class=\"d1\">theological points in Romans were <b>relevant to what was going on in Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">different points in other letters for other situations</li></ul>"},{"t":184.89,"lv":3,"h":"Rome's situation: unity of the two groups","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Background we've covered: <b>expulsion of the Jews</b>, then their <b>return</b> to Rome</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's concern: because of time away, a <b>split into two churches</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one <b>Jewish Christian</b>, one <b>Gentile Christian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he wanted to make sure that <b>didn't happen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Theological sections: what it means to be part of <b>Israel</b> - <b>not the same as being Jewish</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Application section: heavy focus on <b>unity</b> of the community, practically</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>love</b>, helping, <b>bearing with weaknesses</b>, giving up our <b>\"rights\"</b>, no <b>stumbling blocks</b>, loving enemies</li><li class=\"d0\">Argued: you're <b>all one people in Christ</b> → now <b>\"this is what that looks like\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every day and every week <b>as you gather for worship</b></li></ul>"},{"t":296.49,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:1 - The strong bear with the weak","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"We then who are strong ought to bear with the <b>scruples of the weak</b> and not please ourselves\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Refers back to last time: yes we have <b>rights</b>, yes we're <b>free</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but never use them to <b>spiritually or otherwise harm</b> fellow Christians</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>Sabbath</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish Christian goes to synagogue <b>and</b> Sunday Eucharist - great</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentile who doesn't → don't <b>judge</b> him; and he mustn't criticize or talk the other out of it, <b>offend his conscience</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some still kept <b>kosher</b>; Paul: as far as God is concerned <b>everything is clean</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but if someone's faithfulness is expressed in diet - <b>vegetarian</b>, avoiding <b>meat offered to idols</b> in the marketplace - let them be</li><li class=\"d1\">and they shouldn't judge those who don't</li></ul>"},{"t":439.07,"lv":3,"h":"\"Scruples\" and Paul's consistent position","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Translation \"scruples\" doesn't capture the original - \"opinion\" not right either</li><li class=\"d0\">Word used of the <b>Stoics</b> practicing a certain way of life → <b>principles of a way of life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"things you've chosen to do and not do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christian way of life exists already, but <b>variations within it</b> - esp. people from <b>two cultures</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>never</b> says Jews should stop keeping <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">defended himself against that accusation passionately in <b>Acts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">never: trim your beard, <b>eat shrimp and pork</b> to prove you're Christian</li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>violently opposes</b> forcing Gentiles to do those things as a <b>condition</b> of the community</li></ul>"},{"t":549.72,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:2-3 - Please your neighbor for his good","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 2: \"Let each of us please his neighbor <b>for his good, leading to edification</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Underlying principle: Christians <b>don't live to please ourselves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I may love a good <b>pork sandwich</b>\" - don't eat it in front of him; eat what I want at home</li><li class=\"d1\">with him: share fellowship, <b>not purposefully offend</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Please\" ≠ just make happy - sinful things might please someone</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>for their good</b> = built up, helped to <b>maturity</b> - <b>objective</b>, not short-term happiness</li><li class=\"d1\">addict: giving drugs would please - we don't, <b>not for his good</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v. 3: \"Even <b>Christ did not please himself</b>\" - reproaches fell on me</li><li class=\"d1\">came \"not to be served but to serve and give his life a <b>ransom for many</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">came for our good, to save us - <b>not</b> so we'd be \"wealthy, healthy and wise, contra some <b>television preachers</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":679.99,"lv":3,"h":"Psalm 69 and citing by first line","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul cites <b>Psalm 69:9</b> - whole psalm about the <b>Messiah</b> suffering for his people</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>No chapters and verses</b> then → quote a line, and it directs you to the <b>rest</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people had to <b>know scripture</b> much better than we do</li><li class=\"d1\">like knowing everybody's <b>phone number</b> as a kid - now all programmed in</li></ul>"},{"t":750.17,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:4 - Written for our learning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Whatever things were written before were written <b>for our learning</b>... through patience and comfort of the <b>scriptures</b> might have <b>hope</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Scriptures\" and \"things written\" = <b>same word</b> - that's what scriptures means</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"It is written\"</b> didn't resonate then as now</li><li class=\"d1\">today lots written - libraries of <b>utter nonsense</b>; \"pretty much the whole internet, except <b>Ancient Faith</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">napkin note - who cares</li><li class=\"d0\">Then: writing was <b>expensive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a copy of <b>Romans</b> (not that long) ≈ <b>$5,000</b> in today's money</li><li class=\"d1\">written down, <b>copied and recopied</b> at great cost through centuries → like a <b>monument</b>, great significance</li></ul>"},{"t":889.06,"lv":3,"h":"No canon in Paul's \"whatever was written\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul makes <b>no distinction about canon</b> - \"there isn't one\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't say Torah, or <b>Law and Prophets</b>, though he could</li><li class=\"d0\">Constantly quotes literature that <b>didn't end up in most people's Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more to come in <b>1 and 2 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes quotes things <b>we no longer have</b> - hard to tell</li><li class=\"d1\">recovered texts → \"oh, that's what he's referencing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Capital-S \"<b>Scriptures</b>\" (Orthodox Study Bible) makes us think Bible / Old Testament - Paul means <b>wide range of texts, literature and traditions</b> received</li></ul>"},{"t":996.19,"lv":3,"h":"\"Our\" learning: Israel's heritage is the Gentiles'","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Our\" includes <b>Jewish-background and Gentile-background</b> members</li><li class=\"d1\">usually quoting Jewish sources - but they belong to <b>Gentiles just as much</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>grafted in</b> to Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: does he ever quote a Greek or Roman?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Acts 17</b>: \"in him we live and move and have our being\" - from a <b>hymn to Zeus</b>; also <b>Stoic philosophers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>never</b> with \"it is written\" - only <b>traditions of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Our heritage too, any ethnicity: \"<b>God of our fathers</b>\" = Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</li><li class=\"d1\">fathers because we're the <b>church = Israel</b>, <b>not a new Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: all of Israel's literary heritage up to this epistle = heritage of <b>the whole community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">note <b>\"for\"</b> - he just quoted <b>Psalm 69</b> to Jews and Gentiles: \"written <b>for you</b>\" about Christ and how to live in him</li></ul>"},{"t":1156.17,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:5-6 - God of patience and comfort","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"May the <b>God of patience and comfort</b> grant you to be <b>like-minded</b>... one mind and <b>one mouth</b> glorify the God and Father...\"</li><li class=\"d1\">version of \"one mind and one mouth\" used in the <b>liturgy</b> - before the hymn, <b>Kiss of Peace</b>; Holy Spirit added there</li><li class=\"d0\">Be like Christ (v. 3) → now <b>be like God</b>: patient and comforting</li><li class=\"d1\">\"comfort\" = same word as the Holy Spirit as <b>Comforter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">better translation: <b>exhort</b> - building up, doing good, <b>advocating</b> for each other</li></ul>"},{"t":1255.83,"lv":3,"h":"Textual variance in v. 6","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek: \"the <b>God and Father of</b> our Lord Jesus Christ\" or \"<b>God the Father and</b> our Lord Jesus Christ\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">neither heretical; Paul uses the second phrase <b>all the time</b></li><li class=\"d1\">argument both ways: probably what he said - or a <b>copyist</b> assumed it</li><li class=\"d0\">Some say \"God of our Lord Jesus\" implies Jesus isn't God</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul already equates Jesus with God in Romans</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John 20</b>: \"my Father, your Father, <b>my God, your God</b>\" - John presents Christ as God unambiguously</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>incarnate</b> second person of the Trinity; in the Gospels he <b>prays to the Father</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Rare place Paul uses the name <b>Jesus</b> (usually just Christ) → points to the <b>incarnate Lord</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1433.61,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:7 - Receive one another as Christ received us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Receive one another just as <b>Christ also received us</b> to the glory of God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Keeps the theme: be like Christ toward each other</li><li class=\"d1\">received a <b>murderer like St. Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a Jew who <b>fell short of Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>Gentile far off</b>, a pagan</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ received <b>all of us</b> → we receive one another <b>regardless</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1468.88,"lv":3,"h":"Romans 15:8-9 - Servant to the circumcision","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Christ a <b>servant to the circumcision</b> for the truth of God to <b>confirm the promises</b> to the fathers; Gentiles glorify God for <b>his mercy</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"The circumcision\" = Paul's way of naming Christians of <b>Jewish descent and tradition</b> (again in <b>Galatians</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Jews\" wouldn't work - many <b>rejected</b> him; no term \"Jewish Christians\"</li><li class=\"d1\">two groups in the Roman church: <b>circumcised</b> and <b>Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Truth of God</b> = he made <b>promises</b> to Abraham, in the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish background: Christ = <b>fulfillment</b> of promises to your fathers</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles: Christ = <b>mercy</b> of God bringing you into the covenant to worship the true God</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ is <b>both</b> to the two groups</li></ul>"},{"t":1604.59,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:9-12 - Four Old Testament quotes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"As it is written\" again: \"I will confess you <b>among the Gentiles</b>\" - <b>2 Samuel 22:50</b> (2 Kingdoms) and <b>Psalm 18:49</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quote 2: \"Rejoice, O <b>nations</b>, with his people\" - <b>Deuteronomy 32:43</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quote 3: \"Praise the Lord, all you nations\" - <b>Psalm 117</b>; quoted in <b>Vespers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quote 4: <b>root of Jesse</b>, rises to reign over the nations, in him nations hope - <b>Isaiah 11:10</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1626.59,"lv":3,"h":"Gentiles = nations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Gentiles\" really means <b>nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">English translations <b>inconsistent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for a person who's not a Jew, \"Gentile\" makes sense; \"confess your name among the <b>nations</b>\" better</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Why do the <b>nations</b> rage\" - \"Why do the Gentiles rage\" would change the meaning dramatically</li></ul>"},{"t":1722.69,"lv":3,"h":"Deuteronomy 32: God's purpose for the nations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul refers to <b>whole passages</b>, not proof-texting a verse</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deut. 32</b> = earliest of these, <b>one of the oldest texts in the Bible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">really hard to translate - <b>archaic Hebrew</b> words found nowhere else</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>Septuagint</b> translators (3rd c. BC) didn't know how to render chunks - already lost to time</li><li class=\"d0\">Begins with <b>Babel</b>: God <b>rejected the nations</b>, scattered them</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>table of 70 nations</b>, assigned to <b>spiritual powers</b> they ended up worshiping</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>creates Israel</b> - doesn't choose one of the 70; grabs <b>Abraham</b> out of one and makes a nation, brought out of <b>Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Moses' final sermon</b>: purpose from the start = <b>bring the nations back</b> to worship him</li></ul>"},{"t":1863.28,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: how Deuteronomy 32 came to be in the Torah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: must be an <b>old document</b> the writer of Deuteronomy incorporated?</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>Torah came together over time</b> - \"raised on the JEDP\": \"that's going a little far\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems with a rigid \"<b>Moses wrote it</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>language</b> it's written in didn't exist when Moses lived</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>place names updated</b>: \"this place, which used to be called...\" - older name contemporaneous with events</li><li class=\"d1\">someone else wrote <b>Moses' death</b> at the end of Deuteronomy</li><li class=\"d0\">Not that Moses didn't write - but not word for word what we have</li><li class=\"d0\">Deut. 32 goes back to <b>Moses' period</b>, relatively intact - archaic, <b>never updated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">updating ~<b>1000-900 BC</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Conservative scholars: <b>Mosaic origin</b> of the Pentateuch - traditions originate with Moses, then <b>revision, addition, incorporation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2044.71,"lv":3,"h":"Why this fits Orthodox tradition","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not a problem for Orthodox - some say the <b>Greek version is canonical</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but Greek translated <b>300 BC</b> → canonical version didn't exist before that; you must argue for a <b>tradition</b> anyway</li><li class=\"d0\">Fits the Orthodox understanding: <b>scriptures themselves are a tradition</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Old Testament <b>developed over time</b>; the <b>Holy Spirit guides a process</b> as he guided the original authors</li><li class=\"d0\">New Testament different: Paul's letters - good evidence we have <b>pretty much exactly what he wrote</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still the Spirit had to guide it getting from Paul <b>2,000 years</b> to us</li></ul>"},{"t":2138.03,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 15:12 - God's plan all along","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>Israel was not an end in itself</b> - contra his opponents</li><li class=\"d1\">never intended to be God <b>only of Israel</b>; through Israel he <b>calls all nations back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus born a <b>Galilean</b>, technically not a Jew, from this nation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Root of Jesse</b> = descendant of David, the <b>Messiah</b>, king</li><li class=\"d1\">\"he shall <b>rise</b>\" - Paul maybe less metaphorical: read before as rise to be king; Christ <b>literally rose</b> to reign over the nations</li><li class=\"d1\">\"in him the nations shall <b>hope</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Drew on the very oldest, <b>prophets, Psalms</b> → this was <b>God's plan all along</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now at its <b>culmination</b> in Christ and the Christians in Rome</li></ul>"}]});
